          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %strong path integral
            originates in and is mainly used in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
          %p
            The idea is that the quantum propagator – in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            the value of the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mi Cob
                  %mo →
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U : Cob \to Vect
            on a certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            – is given by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+kernel"} integral kernel
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi d
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U : \psi \mapsto \int K(-,y) \psi(y) d\mu
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(x,y)
            is something like the integral of the exponentiated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            over all field configurations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            with prescribed boundary data
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi D
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  K(x,y)
                  \=
                  \\int
                  \\exp\big( \mathrm{i} S(\phi)\big)
                  \\; D\phi
          %p
            and calls this the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong path integral
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \phi
            is supposed to allude to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+space"} measure integral
            on the space of all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
          %p
            The name
            %em path integral
            originates from the special case where the system is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+model"} sigma model
            describing a particle on a target space manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is a path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : [0,1] \to X
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The idea of the path integral famously goes back to
            %a{:href => "#Feynman42"} Feynman 1942
            (and, less famously so, in fact to
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Dirac33"} Dirac 1933
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-relativistic+particle"} non-relativistic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            prescriptions.
          %p
            The central impact of the idea of the path integral however is in its application to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %em definition
            of what the quantum field theory encoded by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            should be, disregarding the fact that in these contexts it is typically quite unclear what the path integral actually means, precisely.
          %p
            Notably the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            summing over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+graphs"} Feynman graphs
            is motivated as one way to make sense of the path integral in quantum field theory and in practice usually serves as a
            %em definition
            of the perturbative path integral.
          %h2#realizations Realizations
          %p
            We start with stating the elementary description of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman-Kac+formula"} Feynman-Kac formula
            as traditional in physics textbooks in
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#ElementaryDescription"}> Elementary description in quantum mechanics
              \.
          %p
            Then we indicate the more abstract formulation of this in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            against the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wiener+measure"} Wiener measure
            on the space of paths (for the Euclidean path integral) in
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#AsAnIntegralAgainstTheWienerMeasure"} As an integral against the Wiener measure
              \.
          %p
            Then we indicate a formulation in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-formalism"} BV-formalism
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#PerturbativelyInBVFormalism"} Perturbatively in the BV-formalism
          %h3#ElementaryDescription Elementary description in quantum mechanics
          %p
            A simple form of the path integral is realized in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Feynman"} Richard Feynman
            and then made precise using the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman-Kac+formula"} Feynman-Kac formula
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            %em
              %a{:href => "#PerturbativelyInBVFormalism"} Perturbatively in BV-formalism
            below).
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+equation"} Schrödinger equation
            says that the rate at which the phase of an energy eigenvector rotates is proportional to its energy:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mi dt
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo =
                  %mi H
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \hbar \frac{d}{dt} \psi = H \psi.
          %p
            Therefore, the probability that the system evolves to the final state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_F
            after evolving for time
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            from the initial state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_I
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
