          %h2#the_path_integral_in_the_bigger_picture The path integral in the bigger picture
          %p Ours is the age whose central fundamental theoretical physics question is:
          %blockquote
            %p
              %em>
                What is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              ?
          %p A closely related question is:
          %blockquote
            %p
              %em What is the path integral
              ?
          %p
            After its conception by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Feynman"} Richard Feynman
            in the middle of the 20th century, it was notably
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
          %p And yet, among all the aspects of QFT, the notion of the path integral is the one that has resisted attempts at formalization the most.
          %p
            While
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} functorial quantum field theory
            is the formalization of the properties that the
            %em locality
            and the
            %em sewing law
            of the path integral is demanded to have – whatever the path integral is, it is a process that in the end yields a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} (infinity,n)-category of cobordisms
            – by itself, this sheds no light on what that procedure called “path integration” or “path integral quantization” is.
          %p
            The single major insight into the right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical
            formalization of the path integral is probably the idea indicated in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"} Dan Freed
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %em Quantum groups from path integrals
                    (
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9501025"}> arXiv:q-alg/9501025
                    )
                %li
                  %p
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Algebraic+Structures+and+Quantization"} Higher Algebraic Structures and Quantization
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9212115"}> arXiv:hep-th/9212115
                    )
          %p which says that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                it is wrong to think of the
                %em action functional
                that the path integral integrates over as just a
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em function
            %li
              %p
                accordingly, the path integral is not something that just controls the numbers or linear maps assigned by a
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                in dimension
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
              %ul
                %li
                  notably: the fact that quantum mechanics assigns a (Hilbert) space of sections of a vector bundle to codimension 1 is to be regarded as due to a
                  %em summing operation
                  in the sense of the path integral, too: the space of sections of a vector bundle is the continuum equivalent of the direct sum of its fibers
          %p
            More recently, one sees attempts to formalize this observation of Freed’s, notably in the context of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+infinity-function+theory"} geometric infinity-function theory
                is used to compute at least something like a path integral in codimension 1 and 2 in the context of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
                QFT;
            %li
              %p
                see
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+as+a+pull-push+transform"} path integral as a pull-push transform
            %li
              %p and something similar or is indicated in section 3 and section 6 of
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Hopkins"} Mike Hopkins
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Constantin+Teleman"} Constantin Teleman
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topological+Quantum+Field+Theories+from+Compact+Lie+Groups"} Topological Quantum Field Theories from Compact Lie Groups
                  (
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0905.0731"}> arXiv:0905.0731
                  )
          %p
            based on material (on categories of “families”) in
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} On the Classification of Topological Field Theories
            \.
